ince people of the case for  independence so it just shows that  we still have work to do and i'll take  your point that i think it's right that  hasn't been waited for  um turnout for example this poll so not  necessarily directly  uh comparable um but you do acknowledge  that  there are concerns within the snp that  what we've seen play out between nicola  sturgeon and alex salmond could have  damaged that support  well i think that the  conflict within any political party is  can often lead to damaging  certainly damaging the reputation and  the the look of the party but  i i don't think that that's necessarily  what is happening just now  um and it touches more broadly on  this harassment inquiry in general and i  think  a lot of people are almost trying to to  force agendas onto these issues  and the real issue at the heart of all  of these  the things that we're talking about is  the women  that you know were complaining the  complainants  and ultimately as politicians i think we  have to ask ourselves  that throughout this saga have we made  it  easier or more difficult for people to  come forward  and and i think that's what the focus  should be  rather than you know internal party  politics as such  and what do you think the answer to that  question is to the  the question of whether it's easier for  women to come forward  oh i i i wouldn't say so no i think  that's  the the way that this inquiry has has  played out  has just been horrendous and i i can't  imagine  the the horror of the the experience  that  the complainants are having just now um  and there's a couple of things to to  make clear that the first thing is  the scottish government did make a  mistake in terms of  how they actually implemented this  policy and it's actually quite  refreshing i think  to see the first minister in particular  put our hands up and sincerely say yeah  no  we could do better and that's what the  point of this inquiry should be  is to figure out how can we make sure 