 well and  just looking at the ministerial code  it's quite clear the scottish  government's ministerial code says legal  advice  should be considered at the earliest  opportunity now on the 31st of october  2018  the government's own lawyer said its  argument in the case against alex  salmond was more likely to fail  than succeed on the 6th of december they  said that the least worst option was to  concede the case  so why did it take until 8th of january  the following year for that to happen  well i mean there's again a few  different things  to consider here the what has been  published so far what the fm made clear  was that there are continually different  legal opinions throughout a process and  what the government were advised  as that we are now starting to see cause  for concern  in our case however we think that the  legal grounds  themselves are sufficient enough to keep  pushing  this so it kind of comes down to in my  view  the semantics and the details of the  actual  the institutions and the you know the  law themselves  and so i think it's important to let due  process uh take its place so  but the the fm has been very clear that  the moment  that it was made clear to her that the  case  had there was no point and pursuing it  sorry how can you say that though that  the moment she thought that  the case wasn't going to be there when  on the 31st of october the government's  own lawyers said the case was more  likely to fail than  of december the least worst option was  to concede the case and yet you didn't  do anything to the 8th of january i mean  it doesn't really sound that way does it  well no  there's also a couple of things to  consider because  legal advice is made up of different  sources so there's a lot of different  things  to consider and the october the letter  that you're referencing  my understanding is that there was still  grounds  for it to be pursued in court because  the distinction that i think was  important that the fm made  